'''''Cyanotis somaliensis''''', '''pussy ears''', is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the family [[Commelinaceae]], described in 1895. It is endemic to [[Somalia]] in East Africa.<ref>[http://apps.kew.org/wcsp/namedetail.do?name_id=235434 Kew World Checklist of Selected Plant Families]</ref><ref>Thulin, M. (ed.) (2006). Flora of Somalia 3: 1-626. The Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew.</ref><ref>{{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20130801225929/http://www.aluka.org/action/showMetadata?doi=10.5555/AL.AP.FLORA.FLOTA014295&pgs=|title=Aluka entry}}</ref><ref>[http://data.gbif.org/species/15376374/ GBIF entry]</ref> Growing to {{convert|12 |cm|in|abbr=on}} tall by {{convert|50|cm|in|abbr=on}} broad, it is an [[evergreen]] [[perennial plant|perennial]] with hairy, slightly succulent leaves and, in summer, three-lobed blue or purple flowers which resemble those of ''[[Tradescantia]]'', a closely related genus.

This plant is valued for its ornamental properties. In [[temperate climate|temperate]] regions it must be grown under glass, as it does not tolerate temperatures below {{convert|10 |C|F|abbr=on}}. It has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/87495/Cyanotis-somaliensis/Details
| title = ''Cyanotis somaliensis'' | website = www.rhs.org | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | accessdate = 5 May 2020}}</ref>
It prefers rather dry conditions in bright light.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://worldofsucculents.com/cyanotis-somaliensis-pussy-ears/
| title = Cyanotis somaliensis | date = 12 June 2018 | publisher = World of Succulents | accessdate = 12 May 2020}}</ref>

